+# Decision: Preview cache eviction policy
+
+## Context
+
+The preview worker renders one JPEG contact sheet per immutable clip revision and renderer version (typically 2 MiB, about 450 ms per render) into a private, bounded cache of at most 12 GiB. Editors alternate between two conflicting workloads: long sequential screenings that touch each clip once, and comparison sessions that re-request a small working set repeatedly. The mix varies by production, so the cache replacement policy materially changes hit rate — and every miss costs a 450 ms re-render.
+
+The policy is an internal implementation detail of the preview worker. The HLD fixes the external contract for every candidate: the HTTP interface (200 / 202 with job ticket / 404), render-queue concurrency, the pending-response protocol, and the disk budget are identical under any of them, and no other node reads cache files or replacement metadata. Selection therefore turns entirely on hit-rate behavior against the observed workload mix, weighed against the bookkeeping the worker must carry.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+- **Scan resistance** *(major)*: a long sequential screening of ~6,000 clips viewed weekly must not evict sheets from an active comparison set. Each wrongful eviction costs a 450 ms re-render of a sheet that was recently used and is about to be used again. Derives from the workload structure in the HLD's problem statement.
+- **Project-switch adaptation** *(major)*: when editors move to a new production, the cache should re-warm to the new working set promptly rather than serving stale hit-rate statistics from the previous project.
+- **Bookkeeping cost** *(moderate)*: per-request state the worker must maintain under the "keep replacement bookkeeping private to the preview worker" tenet — counters, timestamps, and queue membership, all on the request path of a single eight-core worker serving 24 editors.
+- **Tuning burden** *(moderate)*: how many free parameters the policy introduces, and how observable their effect is during the pilot.
+
+All three candidates pass the inherited constraints unchanged (interface, concurrency, budget, privacy), so no gate eliminates an option; the decision is entirely a criteria trade.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### LRU
+
+**Description**: a single queue of sheets ordered by last reference. A hit moves the sheet to the most-recently-used end; admission pressure evicts the least-recently-used tail. One timestamp or list node per entry, no counters, no configuration.
+
+**Analysis**:
+- Scan resistance: **weak** — a sequential screening marches every one-use sheet through the queue, evicting the comparison set behind it; last-reference ordering cannot distinguish a one-use scan sheet from a sheet hit twice an hour ago.
+- Project-switch adaptation: **strong** — the queue re-warms to new work immediately.
+- Bookkeeping cost: **strong** — a single list operation per hit; the cheapest of the three.
+- Tuning burden: **strong** — no parameters.
+
+**Summary**: minimal machinery, maximal vulnerability to the exact workload (screenings) the archive sees most.
+
+### Frequency-based eviction with aging
+
+**Description**: each entry carries a hit counter that decays over time; admission pressure evicts the lowest-count entries. Repeatedly popular comparison sheets accumulate high counts and survive screenings; aging counters down so counts from a previous production fade.
+
+**Analysis**:
+- Scan resistance: **strong** — one-use sheets never accumulate counts, so the comparison set survives.
+- Project-switch adaptation: **partial** — aging eventually clears old counts, but the decay rate trades directly against retention: fast enough to follow a project switch, it also erodes the comparison set's protection within one project.
+- Bookkeeping cost: **weak** — a counter update on every hit plus an aging pass over all entries, the heaviest per-request work of the three.
+- Tuning burden: **weak** — the aging rate is a second free parameter whose effect is only observable over hours, and its correct value varies by production.
+
+**Summary**: the strongest protection, paid for in the most machinery and the hardest tuning.
+
+### 2Q
+
+**Description**: two queues split the budget — A1 for first-use sheets, managed FIFO, and A2 for reused sheets, managed LRU. A sheet enters A1 on admission; a hit while it is in A1 promotes it to A2. Admission pressure evicts the A1 tail while A1 exceeds its share of the budget, otherwise the A2 LRU end. In the simplified variant chosen here, no ghost list records A1 evictions; a re-requested evictee re-enters A1.
+
+**Analysis**:
+- Scan resistance: **strong** — a screening fills A1 and evicts only A1; sheets promoted to A2 are untouched until A1 is drained.
+- Project-switch adaptation: **strong** — A2's LRU ordering re-warms to new comparisons without any decay machinery to tune.
+- Bookkeeping cost: **partial** — two queue memberships per entry instead of one; more than LRU, but purely list operations with no counters or aging passes.
+- Tuning burden: **partial** — one free parameter, the A1 share (initially 25% of the budget), whose effect is directly observable as A1/A2 hit split during the pilot.
+
+**Summary**: LRU's list-only machinery arranged so that screenings and comparisons stop competing for the same queue.
+
+## Selection
+
+**2Q.** It is the only candidate that is strong on both major criteria: screenings and comparisons occupy separate queues, so neither workload damages the other, and a project switch re-warms through A2's LRU ordering with no decay parameter to mistune. The choice turns on the workload structure the HLD names — alternating screenings and comparison sets — against which LRU is directly vulnerable. Aged frequency matches 2Q on scan resistance but pays for it with the weakest scores on both cost criteria: counter maintenance and an aging rate that must be tuned per production anyway, which concedes 2Q's advantage for no gain in adaptation.
+
+Committing to 2Q obligates the worker to maintain two queue memberships per entry and obligates the pilot to tune the A1 share (recorded as a deferred decision in the LLD); the A1out ghost-list variant remains a documented future option if pilot data shows sheets frequently re-requested shortly after A1 eviction. It forecloses single-queue simplicity and accepts that a re-requested A1 evictee needs a second hit to reach A2 in the simplified variant. All of this stays inside the preview worker, per the tenet that keeps replacement bookkeeping private; the external contract is unchanged, per the tenet that every eviction policy returns the same outcomes.
